00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.280 align:start position:0%
 
if you are just one of those players  you've never played on a competitive  team before you've always just played so  low-key you've played ladder and you're  like hey I'm really good League of  Legends how do I get a chance to play on  a pro team  well if you're asking how you get to pro  a lot of people are gonna say Academy  nine Academy will be your 2018 North  American Academy League champion  three-fifths of the Rakata me roster got  promoted for day one here this is kind  of what the Academy system was made for  if they smash on stage they could  solidify their spot on the starting rust  today professional gaming is an  aspiration shared by thousands of gamers  and enjoyed by millions of fans but pro  gaming wasn't always a viable career as  eSports have grown over the years so has  the infrastructure but even with the  advent of Riots own League of Legends  Championship Series the path to becoming  pro still wasn't clear so riot built the  academy League with the goal of  cultivating the next generation of LCS  talent today we're taking a look at the  path to pro for League of Legends fans  who want to climb their way to the top  before this year it was LCS and  Challenger and there was a huge gap  between those two I think I think the  Challenger Series and the LCS team was a  joke generally in the sense that like  the LCS players never saw the Academy  players as any kind of serious  competition they just existed as a lower  league challenger series was essentially  new teams were put together with the  sole goal of this is how you get into  LCS as a team the bottom teams from LCS  would be put in a spot where they had to  fight for their spot in LCS against the  top teams from challenger it was called  the promotion relegation tournament now  in the franchising era there is no more  promotion to relegation we have ten  permanent teams in the LCS Academy  League teams became tied to the LCS  teams it'd almost be like you know your  varsity team and your JV team because at  the end of the Year bottleless JV  players can be brought up to the main  squad if you're looking for a little bit  more power a little bit of diversity in  the play it's very much in a system  where you can say hey we want X  academies big purpose should be to find  these players in North America and bring  them up the joke the twitch chat always  likes to use is na teams aren't even  made of na players because we have so  many imports from EU and so many imports  from Korea and all these other things  what Academy allows you to do is take  home grown in a talent and foster that  talent and build it up to the point  where they can compete we've seen a lot  of these players really improve quickly  really get ready to go Azazel brought  into cloud nine I think of JJ on fly  quest throughout high school I knew this  was something I wanted to pursue but it  was just like a faraway dream in the  distance that I never really thought  would actually happen I played for the  University of Toronto caleda team we  played in the  North American college championship  where we play second that took us to  international collegiate tournaments and  so fly class decided to pick us up for  their Academy team our coach would help  us focus on what we each wanted to  improve on I wanted to have higher game  knowledge and higher level of  shotcalling so that's what he helped me  with and that's what helped me move up  from Academy tell CS fly quest LCS team  was predicted to be ninth place 10th  place somewhere around the bottom by a  lot of people and then they show up they  win against a bunch of the top teams  that nobody thought they stood a  snowball's chance against and they end  up making it into playoffs and that's  what I think again makes it so cool when  you look at a story like JJ's cuz this  isn't a player who came in and went from  collegiate success to academy success to  bottom of the barrel LCS 9th or 10th  place this is a player who went from  collegiate success to academy success to  playoffs in a split where nobody thought  his team should be in playoffs and  that's awesome that's success while JJ's  story is an ideal scenario of moving  from fan to pro the Academy also has a  larger effect on the LCS I like the  teams are rostering 10 players that if  someone's sick they've got a mid laner  ready to go out of the box I think  that's really really useful if someone's  not playing very well they're  unmotivated or for some reason the game  just really doesn't suit them they're  having a really really bad month you've  got someone ready to go to kind of  promote up being able to work with the  Academy guys and help them improve in  the hopes that they can make a name for  themselves they can have an LCS career  is great because I know that I was like  once in those shoes myself and I wanted  to like prove myself and move my way up  I think there's a lot of opportunities  for mentorship some teams have weird  dynamics who are like they're more  competitive but on our team I found it  to be pretty healthy for a lot of our  players like impact is our top laner for  LCS and our Academy top laner is Viper  and both of them just bounce ideas off  each other all the time if you are one  of the bottom teams in the North  American LCS and you're not looking at  Academy as a potential place to find new  talent grow your organization better  your roster and increase your chances of  being one of the best teams in the  future I think you're missing an  opportunity I think right now the  Academy players are all  actual LCS players it's not borderline  LCS players teams like c9 are using  cavity players very well c9 yeah they  were doing poorly even in scrims like  their Academy team was outperforming  their LCS team so they got a shot and  then for the first couple of weeks they  just mixed up the roster and then they  worked out really well for them c9 with  an incredible run this year you may have  noticed a recent influx of traditional  sports teams and athletes investing in  the world of competitive gaming tune in  next week as we talk to team owners and  players about how the business of  eSports is changing  [Music]

